1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:01,510
In 1970,

2
00:00:01,870 --> 00:00:03,630
the typical new home in America

3
00:00:03,430 --> 00:00:05,310
sold for about $23,000.

4
00:00:06,817 --> 00:00:07,237
Today?

5
00:00:07,397 --> 00:00:08,617
About $393,000.

6
00:00:10,118 --> 00:00:11,818
Even after adjusting for inflation,

7
00:00:12,198 --> 00:00:13,938
that's almost twice as expensive.

8
00:00:14,638 --> 00:00:15,798
So what happened to houses?

9
00:00:19,983 --> 00:00:21,303
The best way to measure it

10
00:00:21,103 --> 00:00:22,343
isn't the price tag.

11
00:00:22,803 --> 00:00:24,043
It's how many years of income

12
00:00:23,843 --> 00:00:24,863
a home costs.

13
00:00:25,418 --> 00:00:26,378
In 1984,

14
00:00:26,878 --> 00:00:28,818
the typical new home cost about

15
00:00:28,618 --> 00:00:31,758
3.6 times the typical household's yearly

16
00:00:31,558 --> 00:00:32,238
income.

17
00:00:32,709 --> 00:00:33,409
By 2022,

18
00:00:33,209 --> 00:00:35,109
it was almost 6 times.

19
00:00:35,729 --> 00:00:36,089
Today,

20
00:00:36,229 --> 00:00:38,409
it's still around 4.7 times.

21
00:00:39,783 --> 00:00:41,403
Reason number one is simple:

22
00:00:41,863 --> 00:00:43,623
America stopped building enough homes.

23
00:00:44,194 --> 00:00:45,614
In the 1970s,

24
00:00:45,614 --> 00:00:48,994
builders started about 1.76 million homes

25
00:00:48,794 --> 00:00:49,414
a year.

26
00:00:50,054 --> 00:00:51,714
After the 2008 crash,

27
00:00:52,094 --> 00:00:52,714
that collapsed,

28
00:00:53,194 --> 00:00:55,334
and the 2010s averaged under 1

29
00:00:55,134 --> 00:00:56,054
million a year,

30
00:00:56,334 --> 00:00:58,134
the lowest of any decade since

31
00:00:57,934 --> 00:01:00,214
records began in 1959.

32
00:01:00,765 --> 00:01:01,185
Meanwhile,

33
00:01:01,365 --> 00:01:02,825
the population kept growing.

34
00:01:03,305 --> 00:01:04,125
Fewer new homes,

35
00:01:04,305 --> 00:01:05,005
more people,

36
00:01:04,805 --> 00:01:05,765
higher prices.

37
00:01:07,150 --> 00:01:08,790
So why didn't builders just build

38
00:01:08,590 --> 00:01:09,050
more?

39
00:01:09,510 --> 00:01:10,390
In many places,

40
00:01:10,190 --> 00:01:11,450
they're not allowed to.

41
00:01:11,902 --> 00:01:14,122
Local zoning rules often ban anything

42
00:01:13,922 --> 00:01:15,882
except single-family houses,

43
00:01:16,142 --> 00:01:17,302
require big lots,

44
00:01:17,562 --> 00:01:19,362
and add slow permit processes.

45
00:01:20,042 --> 00:01:21,822
Land near good jobs is scarce,

46
00:01:22,202 --> 00:01:24,042
and every extra rule adds cost.

47
00:01:25,383 --> 00:01:27,163
Reason number three is the price

48
00:01:26,963 --> 00:01:27,603
of money.

49
00:01:28,109 --> 00:01:28,969
In 1981,

50
00:01:29,409 --> 00:01:31,609
the 30-year mortgage rate peaked near

51
00:01:31,409 --> 00:01:33,109
18.6%.

52
00:01:33,569 --> 00:01:35,189
Over the next forty years,

53
00:01:35,309 --> 00:01:36,449
rates kept falling,

54
00:01:36,949 --> 00:01:39,669
all the way to 2.65% in

55
00:01:39,469 --> 00:01:40,729
January 2021.

56
00:01:41,351 --> 00:01:43,411
Cheaper loans let buyers borrow more,

57
00:01:43,571 --> 00:01:45,131
so they could bid prices higher.

58
00:01:46,417 --> 00:01:47,857
Then rates jumped back up.

59
00:01:48,137 --> 00:01:50,337
Today they're around 7.3%.

60
00:01:50,955 --> 00:01:52,195
On the typical new home,

61
00:01:52,455 --> 00:01:53,835
with 20% down,

62
00:01:54,155 --> 00:01:56,115
the monthly payment went from about

63
00:01:55,915 --> 00:02:01,975
$1,285 in 2021 to about $2,161

64
00:02:01,775 --> 00:02:03,075
today.

65
00:02:03,558 --> 00:02:05,738
That's a jump from about 22%

66
00:02:05,538 --> 00:02:08,158
to about 30% of a typical

67
00:02:07,958 --> 00:02:09,178
household's income.

68
00:02:10,417 --> 00:02:11,117
To be fair,

69
00:02:11,217 --> 00:02:12,497
buying was never easy.

70
00:02:13,217 --> 00:02:14,097
In 1984,

71
00:02:14,457 --> 00:02:16,317
with rates near 14%,

72
00:02:16,117 --> 00:02:18,237
payments took an even bigger bite,

73
00:02:18,357 --> 00:02:19,817
about 40% of income.

74
00:02:20,395 --> 00:02:21,795
The difference is that back then,

75
00:02:21,875 --> 00:02:23,635
homes were cheap and loans were

76
00:02:23,435 --> 00:02:24,055
expensive.

77
00:02:24,775 --> 00:02:25,135
Today,

78
00:02:25,395 --> 00:02:26,455
homes are expensive,

79
00:02:26,255 --> 00:02:28,555
and loans are expensive too.

80
00:02:29,817 --> 00:02:31,177
This has split the country in

81
00:02:30,977 --> 00:02:31,377
two.

82
00:02:31,957 --> 00:02:33,397
People who bought years ago watched

83
00:02:33,197 --> 00:02:34,945
their home's value soar.

84
00:02:35,145 --> 00:02:36,685
People trying to buy their first

85
00:02:36,485 --> 00:02:39,005
home face high prices and high

86
00:02:38,805 --> 00:02:40,185
rates at the same time.

87
00:02:41,350 --> 00:02:42,610
So what would actually help?

88
00:02:43,130 --> 00:02:45,490
Many economists argue the long-term answer

89
00:02:45,290 --> 00:02:45,990
is simple:

90
00:02:46,570 --> 00:02:47,690
build a lot more homes,

91
00:02:47,910 --> 00:02:49,270
especially near jobs.

92
00:02:49,814 --> 00:02:51,894
Some cities are already loosening zoning

93
00:02:51,694 --> 00:02:53,114
to allow apartments,

94
00:02:53,434 --> 00:02:54,254
townhouses,

95
00:02:54,474 --> 00:02:55,314
and backyard homes.

96
00:02:55,974 --> 00:02:57,374
Where supply grows faster,

97
00:02:57,914 --> 00:02:59,614
prices tend to rise more slowly.

98
00:03:00,950 --> 00:03:02,990
So houses didn't get expensive by

99
00:03:02,790 --> 00:03:03,350
accident.

100
00:03:04,090 --> 00:03:05,010
Too few homes,

101
00:03:05,290 --> 00:03:05,970
strict rules,

102
00:03:06,330 --> 00:03:07,670
and the price of money all

103
00:03:07,470 --> 00:03:08,930
pushed in the same direction.

104
